/S /H1
/P 98 0 R
/T (Code Explanation)
/E (Code Explanation)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
506 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 864 0 R
/E (In this Arduino program, a laser diode is connected to pin 2 \(LASER_PIN\), an LDR sensor to pin 3 \(LDR_PIN\), and an IR sensor to pin 4 \(IR_PIN\). Two LEDs are connected to pins 5 \(LED1_PIN\) and 6 \(LED2_PIN\).)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
507 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 865 0 R
/E (The setup\(\) function runs once when the Arduino starts. It sets the laser and LEDs as OUTPUT, and both sensors \(LDR and IR\) as INPUT using pinMode\(\). The laser is turned ON, and both LEDs are initially OFF.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
508 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 866 0 R
/E (The loop\(\) function runs continuously. It reads the LDR and IR sensor values using digitalRead\(\).)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
509 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 867 0 R
/E (If the laser beam is broken \(LDR LOW\) or an object is detected by the IR sensor \(LOW\), it is considered an alarm condition. In this case, both LEDs turn ON. Otherwise, both LEDs remain OFF.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
510 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 98 0 R
/E (So overall, the system uses two sensors for detection, and turns ON both LEDs if either the laser beam is interrupted or an object is detected.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
